The env_0072 subprime auto lender scenario is complete in `env/env_0072_subprime_auto_lender/`. All five difficulties pass both audits, and the whole-scenario check `validate_scenario` returns VALID with no warnings.

The company is Harborline Acceptance Corporation, an indirect subprime auto lender, with 7 products. Five of them have a difficulty each, with 2 tasks per difficulty. The other two (service contracts and dealer agreements) are rendered as reference documents only and, as the pipeline intends, are never audited.

| Difficulty (product) | Policy audit | KB audit | Docs |
|---|---|---|---|
| contract_payments_fees_and_servicing (contract) | PASS, round 4 | PASS | 70 |
| cpi_insurance_verification_and_force_placement (cpi) | PASS, round 10 | PASS | 46 |
| recovery_repossession_and_reinstatement (recovery) | PASS, round 6 | PASS | 47 |
| deficiency_balance_resolution (deficiency) | PASS, round 7 | PASS | 50 |
| gap_waiver_cancellations_and_claims (gap) | PASS, round 14 | PASS | 59 |
| vsc, dealer (reference only) | — | — | 17, 10 |

On my own final run, every difficulty passes its 7 gates, every product's docs check prints DOCS OK, and post-publication traceability is clean. No business identifier (phone, email, name, account number, VIN) is duplicated across difficulties.

**Accepted gaps, each logged in `worklog.md`:**
- **Missing tracking and dealer records.** The recovery and deficiency worlds have no insurance-tracking records, and their contracts have no dealer-funding rows, though the charter says every contract has both. The frozen replay check rejects seeded rows that no step of the task reads, and no scored figure depends on them.
- **Blueprint wording carried as written.** Where the blueprint's own wording is inconsistent, the docs reproduce it unchanged. Examples are a stale parameter description in deficiency, and "signed" versus "received" for the GAP free-look refund. None of it moves a scored value.
- **One coarse rule in cpi.** A refund applies only when no arrival channel holds more than one accepted proof; otherwise the standard refund rule governs. I chose this because the rule language can't select a second-latest record, and the auditor accepted it.
- **Minor gap notes.** In gap, one sentence and one unused definition were logged as placement-only and not reworked.

**Coordinator rulings:**
- **Fresh relaunch.** After the first account cut, I relaunched all five instantiators fresh in parallel rather than resuming them one at a time. Their predecessors' `plan.md` files were on disk to continue from.
- **Factor counts not relaxed.** For the thin cpi and gap charters I kept the required factor counts and named which realistic rules and company schedules were allowed. I banned invented ones such as refund shares, tenure grading, and credit-tier deductible splits.

I also saved this run's lessons to memory: two acceptance tests that ended the gap reimbursement's factor-dependency loop, and the fresh-relaunch approach.
